--TEST--
An `is iterable` test answers for an array and for a class reaching either iteration interface, and `is callable` for a closure and for nothing else — rule:types/type-test, rule:iteration/two-interfaces, rule:types/callable-is-a-closure
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/type-test`: neither row asks anything about the declared type.
// `iterable` holds what `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects` accepts — an array,
// an `Iterable` and an `Iterator` — and `callable` holds
// `rule:types/callable-is-a-closure`'s one shape of value, so every subject
// below reaches the test as a `mixed`.
class Steps implements Iterator<int> {
    private uint $at = 0;
    public function advance(): bool {
        $this->at = $this->at + 1;
        return $this->at < 3;
    }
    public function current(): int {
        return 7;
    }
}
class Bag implements Iterable<int> {
    public function iterate(): Iterator<int> {
        return new Steps();
    }
}
class Plain {
    public function value(): int {
        return 1;
    }
    public static function twice(int $n): int {
        return $n * 2;
    }
}
class Counter {
    public static uint $calls = 0;
    public static function subject(): mixed {
        self::$calls = self::$calls + 1;
        return [1, 2];
    }
}

array<mixed> $values = [
    "array" => [1, 2],
    "empty array" => [],
    "iterator" => new Steps(),
    "iterable" => new Bag(),
    "plain object" => new Plain(),
    "fn literal" => fn(): int => 1,
    "first-class callable" => Plain::twice(...),
    "int" => 7,
    "string" => "x",
    "null" => null,
];

foreach ($values as string $key => mixed $v) {
    echo $key, ": iterable ", $v is iterable ? "yes" : "no", ", callable ", $v is callable ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
}

// Agreement over the whole sweep, counted: `iterable` is its three members and
// nothing more, so a row that grew a test of its own would still print a
// plausible answer on any single line. Each interface is written with the
// argument `rule:iteration/concrete-generic-implements` requires, and the walk
// compares the label either way.
uint $agrees = 0;
foreach ($values as string $key => mixed $w) {
    if (($w is iterable) == ($w is array || $w is Iterable<int> || $w is Iterator<int>)) {
        $agrees = $agrees + 1;
    }
}
echo "iterable agrees ", $agrees, "/", Core\Arr::count($values), "\n";

// The two operators are disjoint over every value in the language: nothing is
// both, and the object rows are where a shared "is an object" test would show.
uint $both = 0;
foreach ($values as string $key => mixed $x) {
    if ($x is iterable && $x is callable) {
        $both = $both + 1;
    }
}
echo "both at once ", $both, "\n";

// A chain reads its subject once however many members it walks.
echo "subject: ", Counter::subject() is iterable ? "yes" : "no", ", calls ", Counter::$calls, "\n";
--EXPECT--
array: iterable yes, callable no
empty array: iterable yes, callable no
iterator: iterable yes, callable no
iterable: iterable yes, callable no
plain object: iterable no, callable no
fn literal: iterable no, callable yes
first-class callable: iterable no, callable yes
int: iterable no, callable no
string: iterable no, callable no
null: iterable no, callable no
iterable agrees 10/10
both at once 0
subject: yes, calls 1
